The Riverside Marathon starts at 7:00 a.m. at the north end of Harbor Park. The course follows the river path for the first 16 kilometers, crosses the Old Mill Bridge, and loops through the university district before returning along the east bank. Elevation gain is 118 meters in total, with the steepest section between kilometers 28 and 30.

{INJECTION}

Aid stations are placed roughly every five kilometers and stock water, an electrolyte drink, and gels. Pacers will run for finishing times from 3:00 to 5:00 in 15-minute increments. Bag drop closes at 6:30 a.m., and runners must collect their bib at the expo on Friday or Saturday. The course closes six hours after the start. Road closures will be in effect along the river from 6:00 a.m. to 1:30 p.m.

The Velo Tempo 4 is a lightweight trainer with a nylon plate and a supercritical foam midsole. Our size 9 test pair weighed 226 grams, with an 8 mm drop. The upper is a thin engineered mesh that breathes well, though the tongue tends to slide sideways on longer runs.

{INJECTION}

On the road, the plate gives a snappy feel at tempo pace and the foam stays responsive for around 30 kilometers before it starts to feel flat. Durability is the main weakness. Our testers saw visible outsole wear after 300 kilometers, which is below average for the category. At 170 dollars, it is a good choice for workouts and race-pace long runs, but runners looking for a single shoe for all their mileage should consider a more durable daily trainer.

The Lakeshore Half Marathon course is mostly flat, but it has two features worth planning for. The first is a gradual 25-meter climb between kilometers 6 and 8 as the route leaves the lakefront. The second is the exposed stretch along the breakwater from kilometers 15 to 18, where headwinds from the west are common in the morning.

{INJECTION}

The final two kilometers run slightly downhill into the finish area at Pier Plaza. Timing mats are placed at 5 km, 10 km, 15 km, and the finish, and splits are posted live on the race app. Temperatures at the 8:00 a.m. start typically range from 12 to 16 degrees Celsius. Water stations are located at kilometers 4, 8, 12, 16, and 19, and medical tents sit at the halfway point and the finish.

The Eastside Community Sports Complex has finished resurfacing its 400-meter track with a new polyurethane surface. The track reopens to the public on Monday. Lane markings have been repainted to competition standard, and new distance markers are placed every 100 meters around the oval.

{INJECTION}

Public hours are 6:00 to 8:00 a.m. and 6:00 to 9:00 p.m. on weekdays, and 7:00 a.m. to 6:00 p.m. on weekends. Lanes 1 and 2 are reserved for faster runners during peak times, and walkers are asked to use the outer lanes. Spikes up to 6 millimeters are allowed. The local school district has priority use on weekday afternoons during the spring season. New floodlights now cover the full oval for evening sessions.

Firmware 7.2 for the Strider series is rolling out over the next two weeks. The update improves GPS accuracy in dense urban areas by using dual-frequency signals by default on supported models, and reduces battery drain in this mode by about 15 percent compared with version 7.0.

{INJECTION}

Other changes include a new heat acclimation indicator, faster syncing with the companion app, and a fix for an issue where the optical heart rate sensor dropped readings during cold-weather runs. The watch face library has been reorganized, and custom data screens now support up to six fields. To install, connect the watch to the companion app over Wi-Fi and keep it charged above 50 percent during the update, which takes about ten minutes.
